  "slug": "insight-genomic-29c7",
  "summary": "Pooled CRISPR screens, long-read transcript quantification, and network perturbation now target isoform and non-DE gene-level regulatory effects.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is a shift from gene-level to transcript-level units of prioritization, pursued through perturbation design, statistical modeling and network inference. CRISPore-seq couples pooled CRISPR perturbations with joint short- and long-read single-cell transcriptomics to resolve isoform-level alternative splicing. In differential transcript usage, limma and edgeR pipelines divide out read-to-transcript quantification uncertainty as overdispersion. DENetwork extends prioritization beyond differential expression, scoring genes by in silico knockout impact on inferred information flow and identifying non-differentially expressed genes with functional relevance. Methodological controls run alongside: off-target effects are examined as confounders of Cas13d-based lncRNA screens, and ChIP-wrangler introduces dual-spike-in ChIP-seq normalization that clarifies how RNAPII depletion affects H3K4me3 and H3K27ac. These advances are individually distinct; collectively they tighten links between measured isoform usage, perturbation effects and regulatory inference.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
